# The secure element only answers to the configuration it was formatted
# against, and the japanese sky ships as three handsets whose sets differ in
# the model string and in the certificate FeliCa Networks issued for it: XIG03
# (au), A401XM (SoftBank) and 23076RA4BR (SIM free). One vendor image serves
# all of them, so each set lives in /vendor/etc/felica_<model> and the one in
# use is bound over /vendor/etc/felica.
#
# libinit already names the handset from the board - S88029EA1 is XIG03,
# S88029JA1 is 23076RA4BR - so that model is the default. Any other board has
# no set of its own and keeps what is there; init ignores ENOENT.
on property:ro.product.model=*
    mount none /vendor/etc/felica_${ro.product.model} /vendor/etc/felica bind

# Whatever FelicaParts was told to use, bound on top of the above. S88029JA1
# is also the SoftBank board, and nothing the bootloader hands out tells the
# two apart, so this is the only way to reach the A401XM set.
#
# Lives on system_ext because the property has to be writable by the settings
# app: /vendor and /odm scripts expand ${} through vendor_init, which cannot
# read a system property, and a vendor property cannot be set from an app at
# all. init parses this one itself, so it can read either.
#
# Fires when the persistent properties are loaded, which is after the block
# above. A bind mount is invisible to a file descriptor somebody already holds,
# which is why the settings screen asks for a reboot.
on property:persist.felica.model=*
    mount none /vendor/etc/felica_${persist.felica.model} /vendor/etc/felica bind
